27, 2026\n\nAGENDA NOTICING COMPLETED ON:\n\n/s/ Anthony Fletcher\n\nCERTIFIED (NOTICED) BY:\n\nAnthony Fletcher, Planner\n\nPage 2 of 2; September 2, 2026, Planning Commission Agenda\nPage 2 of 175","ocr":false},{"page":3,"text":"VINEYARD PLANNING COMMISSION STAFF REPORT\n\nMeeting Date: September 2, 2026\nAgenda Item: Public Hearing — To Consider Ordinance 2026-11 for compliance to Senate Bill\n\n284\n\nDepartment: Planning\n\nPresenter:\n\nAnthony Fletcher, Chris Thompson\n\nBackground/Discussion:\nThe purpose of this item is to receive public comment and consider amendments to the Vineyard\nCity Zoning Code, Subdivision Code, and other applicable provisions of the Vineyard City Code.\nThe proposed amendments are intended to implement Senate Bill 284, Local Land and Water\nModifications, adopted during the 2026 Utah General Session, and bring the City’s land use\nregulations and procedures into compliance with applicable state requirements.\n\nBackground\nSenate Bill 284 substantially reorganized and amended several provisions of Utah law governing\nmunicipal land use regulations. The legislation requires municipalities to review and update their\nordinances, administrative procedures, and application requirements to conform with the newly\nenacted state standards.\nThe City’s public hearing notice identified the following Utah Code sections and subjects that\nmay require amendments:\n• Definitions, Utah Code §§ 10-20-102 and 10-21-101\n• Planning Commission establishment, membership, appointment, removal, recusal,\norganization, and procedures, § 10-20-301\n• Planning Commission powers, duties, and training requirements, § 10-20-302\n• Enactment of land use regulations, land use decisions, and development agreements, §\n\n10-20-501\n• Preparation, notice, public hearings, and adoption of land use regulations, § 10-20-502\n• Classification of new and unlisted business uses, § 10-20-507\n• Model homes and open houses, § 10-20-625\n• Structure height regulations, § 10-20-626\n• Review of subdivision applications and subdivision improvement plans, § 10-20-806\n• Subdivision plat recording, required landscaping and infrastructure, improvement\ncompletion assurances, and improvement warranties, § 10-20-807\n• Land use application approval, municipal requirements and limitations, vesting,\napplication checklists, building permits, and certificates of occupancy, § 10-20-902\n• Culinary and secondary water providers, exactions, water-right exactions, and\nimprovement assurances, §§ 10-20-910 through 10-20-912\nPage 3 of 175","ocr":false},{"page":4,"text":"• Land use enforcement and limitations on withholding permits or certificates, § 10-201001\n• Appeal authorities, appeals, variances, burden of proof, due process, scope of review, and\njudicial review, §§ 10-20-1101 through 10-20-1109\n• Detached accessory dwelling units, including qualifying lot sizes, conversions of existing\naccessory structures, parking, setbacks, design, occupancy, and utility requirements, § 1021-304\n• Online publication of land use regulations, fee schedules, subdivision application\nrequirements, and application checklists\n• Related technical, procedural, cross-reference, and conforming amendments necessary to\nimplement Senate Bill 284\n\nProposed Ordinance\nAlthough the public hearing encompasses the applicable Senate Bill 284 requirements identified\nabove, Ordinance No. 2026-11 is the first of three planned ordinances that will be presented to\nthe Planning Commission and City Council.\n\n1. The first ordinance is planned for the detached accessory dwelling unit (DADU)\nprovisions of the bill.\n2. The second ordinance is planned for the rest of the bill’s provisions that require an\nordinance, except those related to water exactions.\n3. The third ordinance is planned for the water right exaction provisions of the Bill.\n\nEach ordinance will be presented to the Planning Commission and City Council as it becomes\nready for consideration.\n\nPlanning Commission Consideration\nThe Planning Commission should conduct the required public hearing, receive and consider\npublic comments, review the proposed amendments, and forward a recommendation to the City\n\nCouncil.\nIn evaluating ordinances, the Planning Commission should consider whether the proposed\namendments:\n• Bring Vineyard City’s regulations into compliance with applicable state law\n• Establish clear and administrable standards for detached accessory dwelling units\n• Maintain consistency among related provisions of the Vineyard City Code\n• Protect public health, safety, and welfare while respecting applicable property rights\n\nStaff will align the City’s application procedures, requirements and checklists with statutory\nlimitations.\n\nPage 4 of 175","ocr":false},{"page":5,"text":"Next Steps\nFollowing the Planning Commission public hearing on September 2, 2026, the Planning\nCommission’s recommendation will be presented to the City Council. The City Council will\nconduct a public hearing on September 8, 2026, and consider whether to adopt Ordinance No.\n\n2026-11.\nOrdinance No. 2026-11 is the first of three anticipated ordinances addressing Senate Bill 284.\nThe two remaining ordinances will be presented to the Planning Commission and City Council at\nfuture meetings once the proposed amendments are ready for review and consideration.\n\nFiscal Impact:\n\nRecommendation:\nStaff recommends that the Planning Commission conduct the public hearing and forward a\npositive recommendation to the City Council for adoption of Ordinance No. 2026-11, subject to\nany revisions recommended by the Planning Commission, City Attorney, or staff.\n\nSample Motion:\n“I move to forward a positive recommendation to the City Council for adoption of Ordinance\nNo. 2026-11, amending applicable provisions of the Vineyard City Code and Construction and\nDevelopment Standards to implement the detached accessory dwelling unit and municipal\napplication checklist requirements of Senate Bill 284, together with related technical and\nconforming amendments, subject to any final revisions recommended by the City Attorney and\nstaff.”\n\nAttachments:\n\n1.\n\nAction Item Matrix - Senate Bill 284\n\n2.\n\nSenate Bill 284 Brief\n\n3.\n\nGap Analysis - Senate Bill 284\n\nPage 5 of 175","ocr":false},{"page":6,"text":"Vineyard City SB 284 — Code Review Action Item Matrix\n\nAction\n\nItem\n\nEffective\n\nVineyard Code Link(s)\n\nDate\n\nStatus\n\n10/1/2026 VZC 15.34.060(2)\n\nDrafted\n\nEnsure development ordinances and development review\n\nAction procedures establish a process for the owner of a legally\n\nItem constructed accessory structure to convert it into a detached\n\n26b accessory dwelling unit, subject to applicable setback and\nbuilding, health, and fire code requirements.\n\n§26(2)(b)(ii)\n·\n\n10/1/2026 VZC 15.34.060(18)\n\n10-21304(2)(b)(ii)\n\nDrafted\n\nAction\n\nItem Ensure the development ordinances do not:\n\n26c\n\n§26(3) · 10VZC 15.34.060,\n\n10/1/2026\n\n21-304(3)\n\n15.12.050\n\nDrafted\n\nDescription\n\nSB 284\n\nReference\n\nDetermine the smallest lot size desired for detached accessory\ndwelling units. The largest minimum lot size allowed by UC § 10Action 21-304 is 11,000 square feet. Ensure development ordinances §26(1)(b) ·\nItem permit a detached accessory dwelling unit on any qualifying lot 10-2126a of the determined size or larger (current standard is 12,000\n\n304(1)(b)\nsquare feet), and update the Community Development website\nto match.\n\n1\n\nRequire a conditional use permit for a DADU if the proposed\n\nDADU is located in a primarily residential zone;\n\n2\n\nRequire more than two on-site parking spaces assigned to a\n\nDADU that is 650 square feet or larger;\n\n3\n\nRequire more than one on-site parking space assigned to a\ndetached accessory dwelling unit that is smaller than 650\nsquare feet; or\n\n§26(3)(a) ·\n\n10-21304(3)(a)\n§26(3)(b) ·\n\n10-21304(3)(b)\n§26(3)(c) ·\n\n10-21304(3)(c)\n\n10/1/2026 VZ